# This stack's own state, in the container it created, under tfstate.tfstate.
# The first apply has nowhere else to put it, so it starts in a local
# terraform.tfstate; once the container exists, move it there. README.md,
# "Why this stack's state lives in the container it manages", says why. From
# this directory, after the first apply:
#
#   terraform state list > state-before.txt
#   terraform output -raw backend_hcl > backend.hcl
#   echo 'key                  = "tfstate.tfstate"' >> backend.hcl
#   cp backend.tf.example backend.tf       # after the output: from here on, commands want init first
#   terraform init -backend-config=backend.hcl -migrate-state
#   terraform state list | diff state-before.txt -    # prints nothing
#   terraform plan                         # No changes.
#
# Every stack shares the container, each under its own key, so read the
# migration's question before answering. Answer yes only when it says no
# existing state was found in the new backend. If it says a state already exists
# there, the key is another stack's: press Ctrl-C, since yes would overwrite that
# state and no would take it over in place of this one's, and change the key.
#
# A 403 (AuthorizationPermissionMismatch) from init means the role the apply
# granted has not reached the blob service yet; it takes a few minutes. Run init
# again.
#
# The migration empties terraform.tfstate but leaves the old state in
# terraform.tfstate.backup, and this state holds the account's access keys. Once
# both checks pass, delete both, state-before.txt and any saved plan file, which
# carries the same keys.
#
# From then on, pass -backend-config=backend.hcl to every terraform init. Both
# backend.tf and backend.hcl are ignored by git, so each deployment keeps its own.
terraform {
  backend "azurerm" {}
}
